{"parentUuid":"4cb80855-1718-4e17-9ab5-fe8e3d50bd1b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8c86a881-7d93-40d7-9bec-944584aad013","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \\\"sharpened, not soothed.\\\"\\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\\n\\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":308},"type":"attachment","uuid":"58bb1728-7468-47e1-81bc-015687ae9aa3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:57.921Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"77409af1-f69e-4460-b6c0-a82a4b31385b","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"58bb1728-7468-47e1-81bc-015687ae9aa3","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"8c86a881-7d93-40d7-9bec-944584aad013","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":355},"type":"attachment","uuid":"aa040e60-3f0e-4225-899f-1e4dac39d07b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:57.968Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"77409af1-f69e-4460-b6c0-a82a4b31385b","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"aa040e60-3f0e-4225-899f-1e4dac39d07b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## Today\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (16:58)\n\n**Context:** Resumed a paused KeystoneOS / claude-skills audit session (AIOS Gate B/C work), then ran the `/mattpocock-skills:handoff` skill to capture state before stopping.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- The skill-roster checker was validated clean: 16/16 fixtures correct, agreement with the real YAML parser on all 42 skills, live roster failing only the 7 disabled skills, and all 4 fail-closed cases exiting 2.\n- A sleep-induced API error plus a DNS failure (ENOTFOUND) cut off a response mid-run; the user said \"resume\".\n- Claude could not invoke the handoff skill itself — that skill is user-invocation-only, so the user had to type `/mattpocock-skills:handoff`.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Three independent items were approved and run: commit/push the checker fix in KeystoneOS, write the Gate B round 2 dispositions, and add Gate B round 2 to the execution log.\n- Handoff doc written to the OS temp dir (not the workspace), linking to plan/log/report paths instead of copying content.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Some plugin skills are gated to user invocation only; an agent cannot start them. Ask the user to type the slash command.\n- Codex quota exhaustion (reset 10:41 PM) blocks review gates — sequence Codex re-inspection around the reset rather than retrying.\n- Staged-but-uncommitted fixes (C-01 executable bit) are easy to lose across a session break; commit before pausing.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Pending user decisions: (1) the 7 skills off in Claude but live in Codex/Pi — ArXiv, ISA, Science, capture-insight, library-review, pp-espn, session-miner — recommend retiring from canonical unless `library-review` or `pp-espn` is used in Codex; (2) Task 8 Pi trial step 1 needs `sudo` — recommend the user runs it.\n- Gate C remaining: commit C-01, fix C-02 (lock race), fix C-03 (usage counts cover Claude only).\n- Codex re-inspection of manifest fix `7459560` after quota reset; then Gate D; then update `docs/STATUS.md`.\n- Commit the BLOCKED Task 8 report at `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/t8-pi-trial.md`.\n- Still-open task from earlier: write the project-level skill at `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` (8-part read-only audit procedure).\n- Handoff file: `/var/folders/gw/8qg6y2bj7rvdqzttmxfl9lx00000gn/T/handoff-2026-09-13-aios-gate-c.md`\n- Last commits: `7459560` (claude-skills), `08bf6e8` (KeystoneOS).\n","## Today\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (16:58)\n\n**Context:** Resumed a paused KeystoneOS / claude-skills audit session (AIOS Gate B/C work), then ran the `/mattpocock-skills:handoff` skill to capture state before stopping.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- The skill-roster checker was validated clean: 16/16 fixtures correct, agreement with the real YAML parser on all 42 skills, live roster failing only the 7 disabled skills, and all 4 fail-closed cases exiting 2.\n- A sleep-induced API error plus a DNS failure (ENOTFOUND) cut off a response mid-run; the user said \"resume\".\n- Claude could not invoke the handoff skill itself — that skill is user-invocation-only, so the user had to type `/mattpocock-skills:handoff`.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Three independent items were approved and run: commit/push the checker fix in KeystoneOS, write the Gate B round 2 dispositions, and add Gate B round 2 to the execution log.\n- Handoff doc written to the OS temp dir (not the workspace), linking to plan/log/report paths instead of copying content.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Some plugin skills are gated to user invocation only; an agent cannot start them. Ask the user to type the slash command.\n- Codex quota exhaustion (reset 10:41 PM) blocks review gates — sequence Codex re-inspection around the reset rather than retrying.\n- Staged-but-uncommitted fixes (C-01 executable bit) are easy to lose across a session break; commit before pausing.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Pending user decisions: (1) the 7 skills off in Claude but live in Codex/Pi — ArXiv, ISA, Science, capture-insight, library-review, pp-espn, session-miner — recommend retiring from canonical unless `library-review` or `pp-espn` is used in Codex; (2) Task 8 Pi trial step 1 needs `sudo` — recommend the user runs it.\n- Gate C remaining: commit C-01, fix C-02 (lock race), fix C-03 (usage counts cover Claude only).\n- Codex re-inspection of manifest fix `7459560` after quota reset; then Gate D; then update `docs/STATUS.md`.\n- Commit the BLOCKED Task 8 report at `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/t8-pi-trial.md`.\n- Still-open task from earlier: write the project-level skill at `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` (8-part read-only audit procedure).\n- Handoff file: `/var/folders/gw/8qg6y2bj7rvdqzttmxfl9lx00000gn/T/handoff-2026-09-13-aios-gate-c.md`\n- Last commits: `7459560` (claude-skills), `08bf6e8` (KeystoneOS).\n","## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6cf33b95-f8e3-4c19-80f5-8cb780316609","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:57.969Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## Today\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (16:58)\n\n**Context:** Resumed a paused KeystoneOS / claude-skills audit session (AIOS Gate B/C work), then ran the `/mattpocock-skills:handoff` skill to capture state before stopping.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- The skill-roster checker was validated clean: 16/16 fixtures correct, agreement with the real YAML parser on all 42 skills, live roster failing only the 7 disabled skills, and all 4 fail-closed cases exiting 2.\n- A sleep-induced API error plus a DNS failure (ENOTFOUND) cut off a response mid-run; the user said \"resume\".\n- Claude could not invoke the handoff skill itself — that skill is user-invocation-only, so the user had to type `/mattpocock-skills:handoff`.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Three independent items were approved and run: commit/push the checker fix in KeystoneOS, write the Gate B round 2 dispositions, and add Gate B round 2 to the execution log.\n- Handoff doc written to the OS temp dir (not the workspace), linking to plan/log/report paths instead of copying content.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Some plugin skills are gated to user invocation only; an agent cannot start them. Ask the user to type the slash command.\n- Codex quota exhaustion (reset 10:41 PM) blocks review gates — sequence Codex re-inspection around the reset rather than retrying.\n- Staged-but-uncommitted fixes (C-01 executable bit) are easy to lose across a session break; commit before pausing.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Pending user decisions: (1) the 7 skills off in Claude but live in Codex/Pi — ArXiv, ISA, Science, capture-insight, library-review, pp-espn, session-miner — recommend retiring from canonical unless `library-review` or `pp-espn` is used in Codex; (2) Task 8 Pi trial step 1 needs `sudo` — recommend the user runs it.\n- Gate C remaining: commit C-01, fix C-02 (lock race), fix C-03 (usage counts cover Claude only).\n- Codex re-inspection of manifest fix `7459560` after quota reset; then Gate D; then update `docs/STATUS.md`.\n- Commit the BLOCKED Task 8 report at `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/t8-pi-trial.md`.\n- Still-open task from earlier: write the project-level skill at `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` (8-part read-only audit procedure).\n- Handoff file: `/var/folders/gw/8qg6y2bj7rvdqzttmxfl9lx00000gn/T/handoff-2026-09-13-aios-gate-c.md`\n- Last commits: `7459560` (claude-skills), `08bf6e8` (KeystoneOS).\n\n## Today\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (16:58)\n\n**Context:** Resumed a paused KeystoneOS / claude-skills audit session (AIOS Gate B/C work), then ran the `/mattpocock-skills:handoff` skill to capture state before stopping.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- The skill-roster checker was validated clean: 16/16 fixtures correct, agreement with the real YAML parser on all 42 skills, live roster failing only the 7 disabled skills, and all 4 fail-closed cases exiting 2.\n- A sleep-induced API error plus a DNS failure (ENOTFOUND) cut off a response mid-run; the user said \"resume\".\n- Claude could not invoke the handoff skill itself — that skill is user-invocation-only, so the user had to type `/mattpocock-skills:handoff`.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Three independent items were approved and run: commit/push the checker fix in KeystoneOS, write the Gate B round 2 dispositions, and add Gate B round 2 to the execution log.\n- Handoff doc written to the OS temp dir (not the workspace), linking to plan/log/report paths instead of copying content.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Some plugin skills are gated to user invocation only; an agent cannot start them. Ask the user to type the slash command.\n- Codex quota exhaustion (reset 10:41 PM) blocks review gates — sequence Codex re-inspection around the reset rather than retrying.\n- Staged-but-uncommitted fixes (C-01 executable bit) are easy to lose across a session break; commit before pausing.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Pending user decisions: (1) the 7 skills off in Claude but live in Codex/Pi — ArXiv, ISA, Science, capture-insight, library-review, pp-espn, session-miner — recommend retiring from canonical unless `library-review` or `pp-espn` is used in Codex; (2) Task 8 Pi trial step 1 needs `sudo` — recommend the user runs it.\n- Gate C remaining: commit C-01, fix C-02 (lock race), fix C-03 (usage counts cover Claude only).\n- Codex re-inspection of manifest fix `7459560` after quota reset; then Gate D; then update `docs/STATUS.md`.\n- Commit the BLOCKED Task 8 report at `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/t8-pi-trial.md`.\n- Still-open task from earlier: write the project-level skill at `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` (8-part read-only audit procedure).\n- Handoff file: `/var/folders/gw/8qg6y2bj7rvdqzttmxfl9lx00000gn/T/handoff-2026-09-13-aios-gate-c.md`\n- Last commits: `7459560` (claude-skills), `08bf6e8` (KeystoneOS).\n\n## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"77409af1-f69e-4460-b6c0-a82a4b31385b","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"6cf33b95-f8e3-4c19-80f5-8cb780316609","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2b2b8c54-8118-4bd6-9751-c0ff709d21de","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**What you do next, on Windows:**\n1. Run `/dev-sync` first, so Windows gets these changes.\n2. In `Desktop\\DEV\\Flint`, run these three commands in order:\n   ```\n   uv run scripts/triage_model_pass.py\n   uv run scripts/jev_pass.py state\n   uv run scripts/jev_pass.py run\n   ```\n   The run skips the 284 files Jev already did.\n3. When Jev is done, I label its answers. Then you approve the shortlist.\n\nOne small item is still open on this Mac: the memory compiler has 1 log waiting. Running `compile.py` by hand clears it.\n\n**User:** the compile script ran on windows do we need to run it here? if so do it\n\n**Assistant:** The compiler folder is shared through GitHub. The Mac copy is 1 commit behind, which is probably the Windows compile. I'll pull, then check what is left.\n\n**Assistant:** The backlog error came from this Mac's 9/30 night run, and it is handled now. I'll clear it.\n\n**Assistant:** Yes, the Mac needed its own run, and I ran it. It worked.\n\n- Each machine keeps its own daily logs and its own articles, and git does not sync them. So the Windows run did not cover the Mac logs.\n- The run compiled the Mac's 3 waiting logs (9/27, 9/29 and 9/30) with no failures.\n- The Mac knowledge base went from 298 to 318 articles.\n- A second check says nothing is left to compile.\n- The run cost $3.68.\n- I removed the compiler error from the error log and pushed the change. The error log no longer has any open entries.\n\nYou don't need to do anything. Flint's next step is the Jev run on Windows.\n\n**User:** <command-message>dev-sync</command-message>\n<command-name>/dev-sync</command-name>\n\n**User:** Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/dev-sync\n\n# DEV sync\n\nEric works on a Mac and a Windows laptop. GitHub (`erash11`, all private) is the transport. This skill makes the current machine match GitHub without losing anything on either side. It is the first thing to run when sitting down at a machine.\n\n## Step 0: find DEV (never depend on cwd)\n\nEric may run this from anywhere. Detect the machine and go to its DEV root:\n\n- macOS (`uname` = Darwin): `~/DEV`\n- Windows (PowerShell, or `$OS` = Windows_NT): `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV`\n\nIf that folder does not exist, stop and say so. Do not guess another location. Print the root you chose on the first line of output.\n\n## Step 1: config and skills\n\nThese are separate repos and the other machine may have changed them:\n\n1. `~/.claude` (repo `dotclaude`): `git pull`. If `~/.claude/machine.md` is missing, copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` for this OS.\n2. `~/.claude/skills` (repo `claude-skills`): `git pull`, then run `sync-to-codex.sh` (Mac) or `sync-to-codex.ps1` (Windows) so Codex sees the shared skills.\n3. `~/.claude/global-memory` (repo `claude-global-memory`): `git pull`. **This is the only thing that carries memory between the two machines.** USER.md, MEMORY.md and the handoffs live here. `daily-backup` pushes this repo nightly, but nothing pulled it at the start of a session until this step existed, so a same-day handoff (worked on the Mac after lunch, picked up on Windows that evening) arrived with stale memory. Pull it before anything else reads it. If the pull brings in commits, say how many and name the newest, so it is visible that memory moved.\n\nIf any of the three has local uncommitted changes, commit them first with `<machine> snapshot before sync`, then pull, then push.\n\n4. **Mirror project memory across the two slugs.** Tier A memory is keyed by a slug built from the absolute path, so every project has a Mac folder (`-Users-kristinarash-DEV-X`) and a Windows folder (`C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-X`) under `~/.claude/projects/`. dotclaude carries both to both machines, but each machine auto-loads only its own. Before this step existed, 43 memories written on the Mac never loaded on Windows (found 2026-09-24). After the pulls above, run:\n\n   - Mac: `python3 ~/.claude/skills/dev-sync/mirror_memory.py`\n   - Windows: `python $HOME\\.claude\\skills\\dev-sync\\mirror_memory.py`\n\n   It copies files one side lacks, lets the newer version win when both have one (frontmatter `modified:`, else last commit), merges `MEMORY.md` indexes line by line, creates the twin folder when a project has memory on only one machine, and never deletes. The older side of any replaced file stays in dotclaude history. Put its summary line in the Step 4 report. If it wrote anything, commit `~/.claude` with `<machine> memory mirror` and push, so the other machine gets the result on its next sync.\n\n   Mirroring is a union: a memory deleted on one machine comes back from the other. To retire a memory for good, delete it from both slug folders in the same commit.\n\nMerge note for `global-memory`: `transcripts/.errors.log` and `MEMORY.md` are both append-heavy and both machines write them. On conflict, keep both sides' content rather than choosing one — these are logs, not settings, and a dropped line is a lost memory.\n\n## Step 2: inventory (always show before changing anything)\n\nWalk `DEV/*`, `DEV/Football/*`, and `DEV/SpecialRequests/*`. Skip `.claude`, `_tools`, `_DRIVE` (a link to the Google Drive primary storage drive, not a project; never init it, never walk into it), `token-dashboard` (someone else's remote), and any folder ending in `_OLD_merged*`. For each folder report one of:\n\n- **repo + erash11 remote**: uncommitted count, unpushed count\n- **repo, no remote**: needs `gh repo create`\n- **not a repo**: needs init + create (use the `new-project` skill's file set)\n\nThen `gh repo list erash11 --limit 300 --json name` and list repos with no local folder. Skip: `dotclaude` (that is `~/.claude`), `SecondBrain`, `claude-skills`, `claude-memory-compiler`, `DEV`, `DexaProject`.\n\nEric pre-approves these operations; the inventory is for visibility, not a pause for approval, unless something looks wrong (a repo with hundreds of uncommitted files, a large file about to be added).\n\n**Group the report by activity, not alphabetically.** Run `~/.claude/scripts/dev-activity.sh` (Mac) or `dev-activity.ps1` (Windows), which ranks repos by distinct commit days over 90 days. List the active ones individually and collapse the rest to a count: \"118 dormant repos: all clean\" is the useful line, not 118 names. About 14 repos are genuinely worked at any time; the rest are archives that happen to live in the same folder.\n\nRank by **commit days, not recency**. On 2026-09-09 a bulk migration gave 43 repos one commit each, after which every one of them looked active by any recency measure. Days-worked is not fooled by that, because a migration touches a repo once.\n\nNothing is archived or moved on the strength of this. Sorting is a reporting concern. Moving a dormant repo into an archive folder would break three things at once: `harvest-dev-docs` reads `<dir>/docs/*.md` without recursing, so archived repos would silently drop out of the memsearch index; KeystoneOS `projects.json`, `memory-sources.json`, `memory-index-paths.json` and `discovery-roots.json` hold DEV paths; and Tier A project memory is keyed by a slug derived from the absolute path, so moving a repo orphans its memory. Checked 2026-09-12. Do not revive the archive idea without fixing all three first.\n\n## Step 3: apply, per repo\n\n1. If uncommitted: put any untracked file over 95 MB and any `.env` into `.gitignore`, then `git add -A && git commit -m \"<machine> snapshot before sync\"`.\n2. `git fetch origin` then `git merge --no-edit origin/<branch>`.\n3. On conflict: keep this machine's version, save the remote version beside it as `<file>.from-mac` or `<file>.from-windows` (whichever the other machine is), stage both, commit. Never discard either side. `.DS_Store` conflicts: just delete the file.\n\n   Save the remote side by **moving the file, never by redirecting stdout**. A\n   redirect re-encodes the bytes; on Windows that silently mangles every\n   non-ASCII character (an em-dash becomes `ΓÇö`) and adds a UTF-8 BOM, so the\n   saved copy is corrupt and every later diff shows a phantom first-line change.\n\n   - PowerShell: `git checkout --theirs -- <f>; Move-Item <f> <f>.from-mac; git checkout --ours -- <f>`\n   - bash: `git checkout --theirs -- <f> && mv <f> <f>.from-mac && git checkout --ours -- <f>`\n\n   Never run `git add -A` while `.git/MERGE_HEAD` exists — it stages the\n   conflict markers themselves and commits a broken file. Stage only the named\n   paths you just resolved.\n4. `git push`.\n5. Missing locally: `gh repo clone erash11/<name> DEV/<name>`.\n6. No remote: `gh repo create erash11/<name> --private --source=. --push`.\n\nWindows note: line-ending noise (`LF will be replaced by CRLF`) is normal. If a merge refuses to start because of phantom modifications, run `git add --renormalize .` and commit before merging.\n\nStale-lock note: if a commit or push fails with `cannot lock ref 'HEAD'`, check\n`pgrep -fl git` (or `Get-Process git`) first. If no git is running and the\n`.git/*.lock` file is zero bytes and older than the session, it is stale from an\ninterrupted run. Move it aside — do not delete it — then retry.\n\n## Step 4: verify and report\n\nRe-walk every repo. Report: DEV root used, config and skills pulled (yes/no), the memory-mirror summary line, repos checked, pulled, cloned, created, and any folder still dirty or unpushed. List every `.from-*` file created so Eric can reconcile them. Finish with one line: clean or not.\n\n## Step 5: pick up where the other machine left off\n\nSyncing git state is only half the job. The other half is loading the context that came with it, and nothing else does this — the SessionStart hook injects global memory, but project state lives in the repo, and Tier A project memory only crosses machines through the Step 1 mirror. This step is the only place that repo context gets read back in.\n\nDo this for the repo Eric names, or the one he is in. If he has not named one, pick from the ACTIVE list in Step 2, not from all 132: take the active repo with the most recent commit and say which you chose. A dormant repo receiving one migration commit must never win this, which is exactly what picking by recency across everything would do.\n\n1. Read `<repo>/AGENTS.md` (or `CLAUDE.md` if there is no AGENTS.md).\n2. Read `<repo>/docs/STATUS.md`.\n3. Read the newest file in `<repo>/docs/handoffs/`, if the folder exists and its newest file is within about two weeks.\n4. If `docs/STATUS.md` does not exist in a repo that has real work in it, say so — that repo is invisible to the other machine and closeout should have created it.\n\nThen report, in six lines or fewer, before doing any work:\n\n```\nPicking up: <repo>\nLast session: <date>, <mac|windows>\nWhere things stand: <two lines from STATUS.md Current state>\nBlocked on: <open questions, or \"nothing\">\nNext: <top one or two from STATUS.md Next>\n```\n\nStop there and wait for Eric to confirm or redirect. Do not start the next task off the back of STATUS.md on your own — it records where work stopped, not what he wants to do this morning.\n\n## Do not\n\n- Do not `rm` anything. Move to a `_to_delete` or `_OLD_*` folder if something must get out of the way.\n- Do not rebase or force-push.\n- Do not push DexaProject or any repo whose CLAUDE.md says its data must stay local.\n\n**Assistant:** DEV root: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV`\n\n**Assistant:** `settings.json` changed on this Mac. Checking it before I commit.\n\n**Assistant:** Something put it back right away. Checking my notes on `dcg`.\n\n**Assistant:** I won't fight it. I'll leave `settings.json` out of the sync and tell you about it. First I'll check that the incoming commit doesn't touch that file.\n\n**Assistant:** Four repos are behind and one is missing. Pulling them in.\n\n**Assistant:** Everything is in sync. One gap: `42Analytics` is new, and no DEV index lists it. Your rule is to update the index in the same turn, so I'll add it.\n\n**Assistant:** Windows already added `42Analytics` to the index. I'll drop my duplicate line and pull theirs.\n\n**Assistant:** Everything is in sync and clean. DEV root is `/Users/kristinarash/DEV`.\n\n**Config and skills**\n- `dotclaude`, `skills` and `global-memory` are pulled and pushed.\n- Memory mirror: 2 files copied, 1 replaced by a newer version, 1 index merged. I pushed this as commit `7ba6200`.\n\n**Repos**\n- I checked 146 repos.\n- I pulled 5:\n  - `KeystoneOS` (3 commits)\n  - `PP_Recruiting` (9)\n  - `TypeSafe_Sandbox` (1)\n  - `Volleyball_GRID` (6)\n  - the DEV root (6)\n- I cloned 1 new repo, `42Analytics`, from Windows.\n- I created no repos. There were no conflicts, so I made no `.from-*` files.\n\n**One thing I left alone: `~/.claude/settings.json`.** Something on this Mac keeps adding a second `dcg` guard hook with a Mac-only path. I removed it once, and it came back within seconds. I left the file uncommitted, so it does not reach Windows. Your existing `dcg` hook still works on both machines. The cause is likely `dcg` re-installing itself. If this comes back, it is worth a look.\n\n---\n\n```\nPicking up: Flint (newest commit, today)\nLast session: 2026-10-01, mac (this session)\nWhere things stand: main has all the work (PR #2 merged). All 73 OneDrive folders are released to Jev.\nBlocked on: the Jev run, which only works on Windows.\nNext: on Windows, run the 3 commands in docs/STATUS.md. Then I label the answers, and you approve the shortlist.\n```\n\n`42Analytics` also feeds Flint. Windows finished tier A waves 9–11 last night.\n\nDo you want to go on with Flint, or work on something else?\n\n**User:** <local-command-caveat>The command below was run directly in Claude Code, not sent to you as a request, and its output goes straight to the user. It's recorded here as context for later messages.</local-command-caveat>\n\n**User:** <command-name>/exit</command-name>\n            <command-message>exit</command-message>\n            <command-args></command-args>\n\n**User:** <local-command-stdout>(no content)</local-command-stdout>\n\n**User:** stop\n\n**Assistant:** OK, I'm stopping.\n\nTwo hook failures are still open. I'm only noting them now and did not fix them:\n- `perch-pull` failed on 10/01 (exit 1). The log is at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json`.\n- `daily-backup` failed on 10/02 for two reasons. `global-memory` was behind GitHub. `42Analytics` is also not in the backup list yet.\n\n**User:** are hook failures still a thing?\n\n**Assistant:** No, they are all fixed.\n\n- **Error log:** it is empty now.\n- **`perch-pull`:** it ran fine today at 4:35 PM, with exit 0 on the first try.\n- **`daily-backup`, memory repo behind:** `global-memory` now matches GitHub.\n- **`daily-backup`, 42Analytics not listed:** `42Analytics` is now in the backup list.\n\nThe backup has not run since these fixes. Tonight's 21:00 run is the first check that it passes.\n\nYou don't need to do anything."},"uuid":"d8d10cea-ad3e-4c9c-9d3c-4f5a16e1c078","timestamp":"2026-10-03T14:52:59.593Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"77409af1-f69e-4460-b6c0-a82a4b31385b","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
